WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.343 --> 00:00:02.478
- If I have somebody in my
company who wants to take risks,

00:00:02.478 --> 00:00:03.986
I say, oh no, no, go work somewhere else.

00:00:03.986 --> 00:00:05.986
There's only one thing you
can do eight hours a day

00:00:05.986 --> 00:00:06.897
and that's work.

00:00:06.897 --> 00:00:08.185
I mean, my friends are
some of the richest people

00:00:08.185 --> 00:00:11.362
in the world and they were a little bit,

00:00:11.362 --> 00:00:12.570
they weren't really together.

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:15.396
People ask me when do you
want this and my usual answer

00:00:15.396 --> 00:00:16.272
is three o'clock.

00:00:16.272 --> 00:00:18.080
Follow your dreams but make
sure it's a the right dream.

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:21.024
You know, if your dream
is to be a serial killer,

00:00:21.024 --> 00:00:22.617
please don't do that.

00:00:22.617 --> 00:00:24.171
If you're going to go
out here and sell it,

00:00:24.171 --> 00:00:25.316
do you use it yourself?

00:00:25.316 --> 00:00:26.584
Does your family use it?

00:00:26.584 --> 00:00:28.257
And if they don't, why are you selling it?

00:00:28.257 --> 00:00:30.824
If you aggravate, you got to go.

00:00:30.824 --> 00:00:31.657
It's that simple.

00:00:31.657 --> 00:00:33.685
There should be something
to do that's useful,

00:00:33.685 --> 00:00:36.101
that something that says,
okay, I did something.

00:00:36.101 --> 00:00:37.741
It's like anything else,
if you ask a customer

00:00:37.741 --> 00:00:40.901
what they need they'll
give you the wrong answer.

00:00:40.901 --> 00:00:43.011
My first question usually is to an MBA,

00:00:43.011 --> 00:00:44.562
how are you going to get over it?

00:00:44.562 --> 00:00:45.395
(whoosh)

00:00:45.395 --> 00:00:47.914
- He's an Indian American
businessman and philanthropist.

00:00:47.914 --> 00:00:51.630
He's the founder and CEO of
Innovations Ventures, LLC.

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:54.931
He has an estimated net worth
of $1.5 billion dollars.

00:00:54.931 --> 00:00:56.729
He's Manoj Bhargava and here are his

00:00:56.729 --> 00:00:58.057
Top 10 Rules for Success.

00:00:58.057 --> 00:01:00.140
(whoosh)

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:02.302
- Somebody asked me,

00:01:02.302 --> 00:01:05.300
I think we were just having an interview

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:08.087
and somebody asked me, well,
should we have risk takers?

00:01:08.087 --> 00:01:11.066
You know, entrepreneurs, risk takers.

00:01:11.066 --> 00:01:14.051
And I said, no, no, no, no.

00:01:14.051 --> 00:01:16.407
Entrepreneurs are not risk takers.

00:01:16.407 --> 00:01:18.571
You know, if I have somebody in my company

00:01:18.571 --> 00:01:20.089
who wants to take risks, I say, oh no, no,

00:01:20.089 --> 00:01:22.604
go work somewhere else.

00:01:22.604 --> 00:01:25.944
Our job as entrepreneurs
is to minimize risk,

00:01:25.944 --> 00:01:30.478
to manage risk, to give it to
somebody else, not take it.

00:01:30.478 --> 00:01:32.370
If you want to take risks, go to Vegas.

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:35.393
(audience laughing and applauding loudly)

00:01:35.393 --> 00:01:38.208
It's a really simple concept
but if you tell people

00:01:38.208 --> 00:01:39.972
you should be risk takers,

00:01:39.972 --> 00:01:42.009
they go out there and
just blow your money.

00:01:42.009 --> 00:01:45.070
I mean, that's, it's really dumb.

00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:48.690
And, again, like I
said, you know our other

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:51.024
principle is please don't do dumb.

00:01:51.024 --> 00:01:54.271
I think there's a misconception

00:01:54.271 --> 00:01:56.321
that if you're a billionaire

00:01:56.321 --> 00:02:00.080
that must be, you must go out
and your butler brings you tea

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:02.161
in the tea garden or something.

00:02:02.161 --> 00:02:04.828
I get up, you know, I get ready,

00:02:06.410 --> 00:02:09.267
pour a bowl of cereal, you know.

00:02:09.267 --> 00:02:10.574
- What's your favorite cereal?

00:02:10.574 --> 00:02:13.970
- I'm not going to start
with that but, you know,

00:02:13.970 --> 00:02:17.857
I put the milk in, I eat
the cereal, drive to work,

00:02:17.857 --> 00:02:19.453
and then I work like hell.

00:02:19.453 --> 00:02:23.047
I work hard, if I can, you know,
if there's work to be done,

00:02:23.047 --> 00:02:24.356
I'm doing it.

00:02:24.356 --> 00:02:26.035
And at the end of the day, I mean,

00:02:26.035 --> 00:02:27.300
it's really not that different.

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:28.976
I mean, there's somebody who said,

00:02:28.976 --> 00:02:31.052
there's only one thing you
can do eight hours a day

00:02:31.052 --> 00:02:32.940
and that's work.

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:36.414
And what if you enjoy your work?

00:02:36.414 --> 00:02:39.038
I mean, to me, it's football,
basketball, cricket,

00:02:39.038 --> 00:02:39.975
all in one.

00:02:39.975 --> 00:02:40.844
- Yeah, wow!

00:02:40.844 --> 00:02:43.677
- Work is great fun and I kind of,

00:02:44.817 --> 00:02:47.975
we've made our business so that it is fun.

00:02:47.975 --> 00:02:52.912
You know, everybody there
enjoys it and we have certain

00:02:52.912 --> 00:02:55.174
conditions in the company
that if you don't enjoy it,

00:02:55.174 --> 00:02:56.416
go work somewhere else.

00:02:56.416 --> 00:02:57.842
- So why did you drop out of Princeton?

00:02:57.842 --> 00:03:00.431
- Well, I was, first of
all, I don't hate Princeton.

00:03:00.431 --> 00:03:01.655
That was a mis-.

00:03:01.655 --> 00:03:02.835
- Misrepresentation?

00:03:02.835 --> 00:03:04.472
- It was a misrepresentation
by somebody who went to

00:03:04.472 --> 00:03:05.577
University of Pennsylvania.

00:03:05.577 --> 00:03:06.917
- Ha ha ha, okay.

00:03:06.917 --> 00:03:09.421
So beyond the college rivalry,
or the school rivalry,

00:03:09.421 --> 00:03:11.094
why did you drop out?

00:03:11.094 --> 00:03:12.245
- Well, I, as I, I mean,

00:03:12.245 --> 00:03:14.666
the Princetonian just
interviewed me a few days ago

00:03:14.666 --> 00:03:15.682
and I wanted to clear the air

00:03:15.682 --> 00:03:16.983
because I never said they were bad.

00:03:16.983 --> 00:03:17.816
- Yeah.

00:03:17.816 --> 00:03:20.210
- And the purpose really,
to me, it was look,

00:03:20.210 --> 00:03:23.185
I got, it was great for
a year and then they said

00:03:23.185 --> 00:03:24.101
why a year?

00:03:24.101 --> 00:03:26.431
I said, look, it's the same
reason why nobody stays

00:03:26.431 --> 00:03:27.735
for five years.

00:03:27.735 --> 00:03:29.006
Why four years?

00:03:29.006 --> 00:03:30.190
I mean, it's an arbitrary number.

00:03:30.190 --> 00:03:31.023
- Yeah.

00:03:31.023 --> 00:03:33.295
- I got what I needed out
of Princeton in one year

00:03:33.295 --> 00:03:36.397
and after that I didn't
think it was useful.

00:03:36.397 --> 00:03:39.591
Partially because, look, you
go to school and the primary

00:03:39.591 --> 00:03:41.229
reason is you want to make some money,

00:03:41.229 --> 00:03:44.044
you want to get a good job
and you make some money.

00:03:44.044 --> 00:03:45.799
It wasn't important to me.

00:03:45.799 --> 00:03:47.929
I mean, I thought, I mean,
my friends were some of the

00:03:47.929 --> 00:03:51.569
richest people in the world
and they were a little bit,

00:03:51.569 --> 00:03:54.352
they weren't really together,
they were sort of messed up,

00:03:54.352 --> 00:03:55.705
a lot of them.

00:03:55.705 --> 00:03:57.135
And I thought, what is this?

00:03:57.135 --> 00:03:59.085
I'm trying to be like them?

00:03:59.085 --> 00:04:00.433
That makes no sense.

00:04:00.433 --> 00:04:03.957
So then I thought, okay, I don't need this

00:04:03.957 --> 00:04:05.961
and let me find something
that's more useful.

00:04:05.961 --> 00:04:10.303
Planning is, I mean, it has
to be related to something.

00:04:10.303 --> 00:04:13.541
In other words, if it's product, is it,

00:04:13.541 --> 00:04:14.605
so you have to look at it that way.

00:04:14.605 --> 00:04:18.759
We look at it simple, if
a product idea comes in,

00:04:18.759 --> 00:04:23.432
I can't plan for a product idea
to come in that's slam dunk.

00:04:23.432 --> 00:04:25.643
If a product idea comes in,

00:04:25.643 --> 00:04:27.727
we're ready to go execute right now.

00:04:27.727 --> 00:04:31.827
And the way we do it is, on
the planning side of execution,

00:04:31.827 --> 00:04:33.264
you know, a bunch of stuff they say, well,

00:04:33.264 --> 00:04:36.528
this is going to take six
months and I don't allow that

00:04:36.528 --> 00:04:38.898
because what people do
is wait five months,

00:04:38.898 --> 00:04:42.074
there's five and a half
months, and then start.

00:04:42.074 --> 00:04:45.308
I want to know, when people
ask me when do you want this,

00:04:45.308 --> 00:04:49.006
and my usual answer is three o'clock.

00:04:49.006 --> 00:04:52.409
And everyday my view is the next day,

00:04:52.409 --> 00:04:54.242
what can you do today?

00:04:55.112 --> 00:04:57.862
Do your maximum right now, today.

00:04:58.795 --> 00:05:00.444
And so, unfortunately,

00:05:00.444 --> 00:05:03.147
some planning can actually go backwards

00:05:03.147 --> 00:05:05.053
because they say, well, you know,

00:05:05.053 --> 00:05:06.867
everybody else takes a year.

00:05:06.867 --> 00:05:08.919
How is that relevant?

00:05:08.919 --> 00:05:12.479
To me it's like, no, no, three
days, four days, let's go.

00:05:12.479 --> 00:05:14.478
So there's a lot of areas in which

00:05:14.478 --> 00:05:16.954
planning is actually counterproductive.

00:05:16.954 --> 00:05:21.837
The problem with everyone is
they follow fashion, right?

00:05:21.837 --> 00:05:25.184
You do what's everybody, all
your peers, think is cool,

00:05:25.184 --> 00:05:27.784
plenty things, you know,
this is, right now it's cool

00:05:27.784 --> 00:05:30.419
to start a business in
Silicon Valley and get rich.

00:05:30.419 --> 00:05:31.315
That's the.

00:05:31.315 --> 00:05:32.148
- Cool trend.

00:05:32.148 --> 00:05:36.220
- When I was in the early,
late 60s, early 70s,

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:38.224
it was entirely the opposite.

00:05:38.224 --> 00:05:42.110
It was get out, drop out,
do something else, right?

00:05:42.110 --> 00:05:44.451
So people tend to, both are have,

00:05:44.451 --> 00:05:48.301
both are really irrelevant,
you know, in the sense that

00:05:48.301 --> 00:05:52.933
fashion isn't what should
define your life, right?

00:05:52.933 --> 00:05:55.150
What everybody does, what everybody says,

00:05:55.150 --> 00:05:57.722
shouldn't define life.

00:05:57.722 --> 00:06:02.227
The ideal thing is to do
something that has value, right?

00:06:02.227 --> 00:06:03.294
People say follow your dreams.

00:06:03.294 --> 00:06:06.173
I think of one game, one
time I gave a speech, I said,

00:06:06.173 --> 00:06:08.592
follow your dreams but make
sure it's the right dream.

00:06:08.592 --> 00:06:11.051
You know, if your dream
is to be a serial killer,

00:06:11.051 --> 00:06:12.903
please don't do that, right?

00:06:12.903 --> 00:06:13.736
- Yeah, please don't.

00:06:13.736 --> 00:06:17.443
- So it's sort of think with your head.

00:06:17.443 --> 00:06:20.648
Although I know movies say,
no, think with your heart,

00:06:20.648 --> 00:06:23.117
no, think with your head.

00:06:23.117 --> 00:06:27.284
Do that which is, you know, has
strength, which has meaning.

00:06:28.366 --> 00:06:32.137
And if you do that which has
meaning, your life is better.

00:06:32.137 --> 00:06:36.550
Most businesses are the
same whether you're selling

00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:39.731
energy products or any consumer products.

00:06:39.731 --> 00:06:43.995
If it's useful to people,
the rest is just business.

00:06:43.995 --> 00:06:47.015
So if you find something
or invent something

00:06:47.015 --> 00:06:51.648
that's really useful, at that
point, it's really common,

00:06:51.648 --> 00:06:55.815
whatever you're selling, the
process is not that different.

00:06:57.485 --> 00:06:59.529
And our process is maybe
a little bit different

00:06:59.529 --> 00:07:02.434
because we really don't
have MBA type processing.

00:07:02.434 --> 00:07:04.931
We basically commonsense.

00:07:04.931 --> 00:07:07.781
You know, we look at businesses
really in a simple way.

00:07:07.781 --> 00:07:10.259
You make good stuff and you sell it.

00:07:10.259 --> 00:07:13.295
You know, instead of, you know,
all the high-faluting words,

00:07:13.295 --> 00:07:17.378
which I really don't
understand, like brand awareness

00:07:17.378 --> 00:07:21.309
and brand equity and, you know,
all the fancy terminologies

00:07:21.309 --> 00:07:23.958
people use that they don't have a product.

00:07:23.958 --> 00:07:28.125
So, what we did was we
simply stick to fundamentals,

00:07:29.764 --> 00:07:33.034
which is, you make a really good product

00:07:33.034 --> 00:07:36.006
and 80% of marketing is
making a really good product.

00:07:36.006 --> 00:07:40.605
And then you go out there
and if people like it,

00:07:40.605 --> 00:07:42.579
and they agree with you, then they buy it.

00:07:42.579 --> 00:07:45.522
Actually you'll find most of
the products that they say

00:07:45.522 --> 00:07:47.439
are good, are marginal.

00:07:48.286 --> 00:07:49.525
Right?

00:07:49.525 --> 00:07:52.440
Most of the folks that come
up with products usually use

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:53.799
some kind of a gimmick.

00:07:53.799 --> 00:07:57.285
In other words, gee, let's
make it pink if it's for women.

00:07:57.285 --> 00:08:01.452
Or let's make what's his name
famous person endorse it.

00:08:03.570 --> 00:08:06.736
Products themselves are marginal.

00:08:06.736 --> 00:08:08.736
And then they say, well,
no, it's a good product.

00:08:08.736 --> 00:08:11.527
Well, my question is, do you use it?

00:08:11.527 --> 00:08:12.759
If you're going to go
out there and sell it,

00:08:12.759 --> 00:08:13.992
do you use it yourself?

00:08:13.992 --> 00:08:15.466
Does your family use it?

00:08:15.466 --> 00:08:17.889
And if they don't, why are you selling it?

00:08:17.889 --> 00:08:20.451
You know, and so really you'll
find most of the fellows

00:08:20.451 --> 00:08:23.081
that say we have great products
but we didn't get success,

00:08:23.081 --> 00:08:25.005
is they really don't use the product.

00:08:25.005 --> 00:08:28.860
They think everybody else should
but not us, we're superior,

00:08:28.860 --> 00:08:30.341
to our own product.

00:08:30.341 --> 00:08:35.146
So, there's a compromise
amongst people about the quality

00:08:35.146 --> 00:08:36.905
of what they sell.

00:08:36.905 --> 00:08:40.104
And quality's also misunderstood.

00:08:40.104 --> 00:08:42.697
In other words, quality
starts if a customer

00:08:42.697 --> 00:08:46.697
doesn't need it, if it
doesn't provide something

00:08:47.907 --> 00:08:50.292
that they're going to pay for.

00:08:50.292 --> 00:08:52.636
We have different technical terms.

00:08:52.636 --> 00:08:55.340
I mean, our technical terms are slam dunk.

00:08:55.340 --> 00:08:56.989
You know, everybody
understands what that is.

00:08:56.989 --> 00:09:00.191
You got a slam dunk product
or do you have a good product?

00:09:00.191 --> 00:09:03.478
For us, we get pitched
several products a week.

00:09:03.478 --> 00:09:05.415
And if it's a good product,

00:09:05.415 --> 00:09:06.976
we don't go out there and sell it.

00:09:06.976 --> 00:09:08.785
It's got to be slam dunk.

00:09:08.785 --> 00:09:12.429
It's got to be where
nobody will refuse it.

00:09:12.429 --> 00:09:14.897
And then you have a hard time with it.

00:09:14.897 --> 00:09:19.064
So, the flaw is really they
don't have good products.

00:09:20.073 --> 00:09:23.795
Definition of a good
product is it will do well.

00:09:23.795 --> 00:09:26.326
Now, there's some amount of execution

00:09:26.326 --> 00:09:30.493
but I would say that if you
don't have a great product,

00:09:31.810 --> 00:09:34.635
any amount of execution is only going to

00:09:34.635 --> 00:09:36.468
buy you a little time.

00:09:37.308 --> 00:09:39.076
After that, everybody will figure out

00:09:39.076 --> 00:09:42.397
that this is not that good
and not buy it anymore.

00:09:42.397 --> 00:09:44.768
We have what I call when I hire somebody

00:09:44.768 --> 00:09:47.268
or that's going to join our firm,

00:09:48.919 --> 00:09:51.827
we have what's called the prime directive,

00:09:51.827 --> 00:09:54.526
which is if any of you
have ever seen Star Trek

00:09:54.526 --> 00:09:56.536
there's a prime directive.

00:09:56.536 --> 00:10:00.764
And the prime directive in
our company is no aggravation.

00:10:00.764 --> 00:10:03.508
Okay, nobody gets to give us aggravation.

00:10:03.508 --> 00:10:06.668
Whether it's customers,
vendors, employees.

00:10:06.668 --> 00:10:08.966
If you aggravate, you got to go.

00:10:08.966 --> 00:10:10.566
It's that simple.

00:10:10.566 --> 00:10:13.193
Because, and it's really business,

00:10:13.193 --> 00:10:16.110
because usually it's the
one percent, you know,

00:10:16.110 --> 00:10:18.455
some customer that's got
one percent of your business

00:10:18.455 --> 00:10:20.179
and he's driving you nuts.

00:10:20.179 --> 00:10:22.681
And he's taking up 80% of your head.

00:10:22.681 --> 00:10:24.240
How is that business?

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:27.509
So, aggravation is the
largest cost in business.

00:10:27.509 --> 00:10:29.522
To me it's really simple.

00:10:29.522 --> 00:10:32.726
We live here at the work,
if an employee is such

00:10:32.726 --> 00:10:35.190
that they're going to
cause all kinds of havoc,

00:10:35.190 --> 00:10:37.606
that they're going to
aggravate, we need to go.

00:10:37.606 --> 00:10:41.334
One time one of our senior
guys yelled at a receptionist.

00:10:41.334 --> 00:10:45.360
So I called him in, I said,
look, you can't yell at her.

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:46.670
You can fire her.

00:10:46.670 --> 00:10:49.137
If she's that bad, they need to go.

00:10:49.137 --> 00:10:53.156
But, we live here, don't
mess up area, this place.

00:10:53.156 --> 00:10:57.417
And I've found that aggravation
is, if you avoid it,

00:10:57.417 --> 00:11:00.904
not only do you have fun, but it is,

00:11:00.904 --> 00:11:03.930
it's a great place to
work and nobody quits.

00:11:03.930 --> 00:11:07.135
I mean, we can't get people to quit.

00:11:07.135 --> 00:11:09.022
We have basically, I mean,
out of those 70 people,

00:11:09.022 --> 00:11:13.189
I think we lose about one a
year and it's to a non-job.

00:11:14.112 --> 00:11:16.280
You know, it's like, okay, they
don't want to work anymore,

00:11:16.280 --> 00:11:18.542
they're going to three days a week.

00:11:18.542 --> 00:11:21.006
We have almost no turnover

00:11:21.006 --> 00:11:23.786
because there's no aggravation there.

00:11:23.786 --> 00:11:28.240
So there's huge benefits
come from a simple concept.

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:30.742
Most of the people that watch this

00:11:30.742 --> 00:11:33.225
are relatively rich people, right?

00:11:33.225 --> 00:11:37.945
I mean, and most of them
are going to earn a living.

00:11:37.945 --> 00:11:42.052
They'll eat, they'll live,
you know, they'll be fine.

00:11:42.052 --> 00:11:45.292
Beyond that, there should be
something to do that's useful,

00:11:45.292 --> 00:11:48.017
that something that says,
okay, I did something.

00:11:48.017 --> 00:11:51.632
I didn't just work on a door
latch all my life or something,

00:11:51.632 --> 00:11:53.858
you know, not to say door latches are bad.

00:11:53.858 --> 00:11:56.640
I mean, I'm sure guys work on
door latches and it's awesome

00:11:56.640 --> 00:11:58.969
but at the end of your
life you want to say

00:11:58.969 --> 00:12:02.091
did I do something of value?

00:12:02.091 --> 00:12:03.945
Or in the beginning, or in the middle.

00:12:03.945 --> 00:12:06.167
You know, if everyday you
go to work and it's like

00:12:06.167 --> 00:12:10.334
Office Space, you know, where,
do I care, does anybody care?

00:12:12.705 --> 00:12:17.307
If that's what it's going
to be then get paid less,

00:12:17.307 --> 00:12:18.664
do something useful.

00:12:18.664 --> 00:12:20.844
You're going to be way better off.

00:12:20.844 --> 00:12:24.094
And chasing money, I never chase money.

00:12:25.198 --> 00:12:26.271
You know, people ask me, well,

00:12:26.271 --> 00:12:28.777
when do you decide to give it away?

00:12:28.777 --> 00:12:32.819
I never, the problem is,
I never was interested

00:12:32.819 --> 00:12:34.069
in having toys.

00:12:35.201 --> 00:12:38.243
But, it's one of those, if you're trying,

00:12:38.243 --> 00:12:39.269
don't try it happens.

00:12:39.269 --> 00:12:40.292
- Right.

00:12:40.292 --> 00:12:42.137
- And the cool factor, you said.

00:12:42.137 --> 00:12:42.970
- Right.

00:12:42.970 --> 00:12:44.613
- It's sort of, when I was young anyway,

00:12:44.613 --> 00:12:46.501
if you were trying to
be cool, you weren't.

00:12:46.501 --> 00:12:48.064
- Ha ha ha ha.

00:12:48.064 --> 00:12:48.897
- Right?

00:12:48.897 --> 00:12:50.079
- Right.

00:12:50.079 --> 00:12:54.162
- So it's sort of if you
do that which has value,

00:12:55.286 --> 00:12:56.710
you're much better off.

00:12:56.710 --> 00:12:59.261
The first step of thinking
things through is to go

00:12:59.261 --> 00:13:01.641
to the customers and
find out what they need.

00:13:01.641 --> 00:13:03.609
Don't just ask them.

00:13:03.609 --> 00:13:04.442
It's like anything else.

00:13:04.442 --> 00:13:07.217
If you ask a customer what they need,

00:13:07.217 --> 00:13:09.186
they'll give you the wrong answer.

00:13:09.186 --> 00:13:13.343
You have to go there and really
think through what happened.

00:13:13.343 --> 00:13:16.352
And then you'll see what's
really needed is so obvious.

00:13:16.352 --> 00:13:19.115
We have, I mean, we have our
own jargon to some extent.

00:13:19.115 --> 00:13:22.401
Like, for example, somebody
comes to me with a project

00:13:22.401 --> 00:13:26.743
or a product that we're going
to go sell or some project,

00:13:26.743 --> 00:13:29.883
I ask them, is it slam dunk?

00:13:29.883 --> 00:13:32.375
You know, and, no, it's really good.

00:13:32.375 --> 00:13:35.037
I say, no, no, is it slam dunk?

00:13:35.037 --> 00:13:37.778
So, but, but, it really,
it's a good product.

00:13:37.778 --> 00:13:42.375
And what it does is it
totally clarifies their mind.

00:13:42.375 --> 00:13:44.330
That, oh, no, it's not slam dunk.

00:13:44.330 --> 00:13:48.017
Then I say, well, why
are we going to do it?

00:13:48.017 --> 00:13:49.737
So our standards are high.

00:13:49.737 --> 00:13:54.416
We have these simple things
which clarify the mind,

00:13:54.416 --> 00:13:57.833
not totally, you know, mess up your mind,

00:13:58.810 --> 00:14:02.667
or have so much jargon that nobody knows

00:14:02.667 --> 00:14:04.405
what your talking about but yet you've got

00:14:04.405 --> 00:14:05.238
all the right words.

00:14:05.238 --> 00:14:08.319
Like ecosystem, whatever, you know,

00:14:08.319 --> 00:14:10.735
everybody's got ecosystems here, you know.

00:14:10.735 --> 00:14:13.737
Or, you know, value propositions.

00:14:13.737 --> 00:14:15.907
No, no, no guys, just
sell good stuff to people

00:14:15.907 --> 00:14:18.058
who need it, you know?

00:14:18.058 --> 00:14:22.652
So, all of that jargon, all
it does is bury common sense.

00:14:22.652 --> 00:14:26.134
So the idea is that you get simple.

00:14:26.134 --> 00:14:27.857
Somebody asked me, you know,
in the interview, I said,

00:14:27.857 --> 00:14:30.148
what does an entrepreneur really need?

00:14:30.148 --> 00:14:31.988
So I said, only two things.

00:14:31.988 --> 00:14:34.572
Common sense and a sense of urgency.

00:14:34.572 --> 00:14:36.208
That's it.

00:14:36.208 --> 00:14:38.703
And they asked me, who should
we learn this stuff for.

00:14:38.703 --> 00:14:41.370
And my answer was from your mom.

00:14:42.717 --> 00:14:46.322
Because she's probably
done more management

00:14:46.322 --> 00:14:47.923
than your MBA professor.

00:14:47.923 --> 00:14:52.090
(audience laughing and applauding loudly)

00:14:54.430 --> 00:14:57.512
Because she's got a budget,

00:14:57.512 --> 00:14:59.240
she's got all these kids running around,

00:14:59.240 --> 00:15:02.924
hard to manage, all of this
thing has to be done everyday,

00:15:02.924 --> 00:15:04.593
seven days a week.

00:15:04.593 --> 00:15:06.971
Now that's work, that's hard work.

00:15:06.971 --> 00:15:09.308
That's learning on the job.

00:15:09.308 --> 00:15:10.790
And people say, oh,
she's just a homemaker,

00:15:10.790 --> 00:15:12.352
that's like a lowly thing.

00:15:12.352 --> 00:15:13.756
No it's not!

00:15:13.756 --> 00:15:17.586
That's great media and people say, oh no,

00:15:17.586 --> 00:15:19.103
you're saying, well, women
should be homemakers.

00:15:19.103 --> 00:15:20.832
No, no, that's the most, that's
the hardest work there is,

00:15:20.832 --> 00:15:22.553
that's the most talent you need.

00:15:22.553 --> 00:15:23.535
(audience applauding)

00:15:23.535 --> 00:15:25.672
Ask the guy who goes out
there and works eight hours,

00:15:25.672 --> 00:15:27.505
any idiot can do that.

00:15:29.162 --> 00:15:32.995
So it's sort of thinking
based on description.

00:15:34.453 --> 00:15:36.709
In other words, if you look at any word

00:15:36.709 --> 00:15:39.703
and you really think about
it, then you understand it.

00:15:39.703 --> 00:15:43.794
Otherwise, you just use these
words that everybody uses

00:15:43.794 --> 00:15:45.925
and they think, okay, now
this guy must know something,

00:15:45.925 --> 00:15:49.078
after all, he knows what an ecosystem is.

00:15:49.078 --> 00:15:52.161
(audience chuckling)

00:15:53.550 --> 00:15:56.337
Our approach to things,
like I said, is different.

00:15:56.337 --> 00:15:58.707
You may agree with it, I'm
sure I'm going to get some MBA

00:15:58.707 --> 00:16:01.560
professor to, I mean, one time the U of M,

00:16:01.560 --> 00:16:04.270
University of Michigan Law
School called me to speak.

00:16:04.270 --> 00:16:05.296
I said, are you guys kidding?

00:16:05.296 --> 00:16:08.546
(audience laughing loudly)

00:16:08.546 --> 00:16:10.406
So I said, you know, I think of MBAs,

00:16:10.406 --> 00:16:13.568
I think it's totally useless.

00:16:13.568 --> 00:16:15.000
So he said, no, no, come over.

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:17.890
So I said, fine, if that's what you want.

00:16:17.890 --> 00:16:19.390
(audience laughing loudly)

00:16:19.390 --> 00:16:21.397
So I went over there
and kind of defined it.

00:16:21.397 --> 00:16:23.729
I said, look, I mean, I'm
not saying that they don't

00:16:23.729 --> 00:16:25.240
teach anything of value.

00:16:25.240 --> 00:16:29.250
I mean, I think they teach
this much that is useful.

00:16:29.250 --> 00:16:31.926
Then teach this much that is useless.

00:16:31.926 --> 00:16:34.175
And then they teach this
much that's harmful.

00:16:34.175 --> 00:16:35.613
(audience laughing loudly)

00:16:35.613 --> 00:16:39.780
So, overall, you know,
averages on the useless side.

00:16:41.027 --> 00:16:43.942
Because you come out of
school and you actually think

00:16:43.942 --> 00:16:45.820
you know something?

00:16:45.820 --> 00:16:46.653
Really?

00:16:47.873 --> 00:16:50.658
You know, and that's dangerous,
because you really don't.

00:16:50.658 --> 00:16:52.142
(audience chuckling)

00:16:52.142 --> 00:16:54.927
So, I never hire people
out of fancy schools

00:16:54.927 --> 00:16:56.359
for that reason.

00:16:56.359 --> 00:16:58.976
My first question usually is to an MBA,

00:16:58.976 --> 00:17:00.608
how are you going to get over it?

00:17:00.608 --> 00:17:04.775
(audience laughing and applauding loudly)

00:17:07.428 --> 00:17:11.325
So, and I'm not, I'm
really not alone in this.

00:17:11.325 --> 00:17:13.418
The good guys I've met, the
chief executives I've met

00:17:13.418 --> 00:17:17.267
of really large companies that
are, you know, that don't,

00:17:17.267 --> 00:17:19.524
usually they get in front
of a podium and it's all PC,

00:17:19.524 --> 00:17:22.305
lawyer, you know, lawyer-cleared stuff,

00:17:22.305 --> 00:17:23.700
so they don't say anything.

00:17:23.700 --> 00:17:26.859
But the good guys that I've
met, they're all sick of MBAs.

00:17:26.859 --> 00:17:27.692
Really.

00:17:28.540 --> 00:17:30.883
They're not really enamored
of this whole thing

00:17:30.883 --> 00:17:33.551
because, you know, look,
it's a simple thing.

00:17:33.551 --> 00:17:35.322
If you're going to learn plumbing,

00:17:35.322 --> 00:17:37.332
go learn from a plumber
that's actually seen a pipe.

00:17:37.332 --> 00:17:39.258
(audience laughing)

00:17:39.258 --> 00:17:42.411
Has fixed a leak, not
just written about pipes,

00:17:42.411 --> 00:17:45.933
and lectured on pipes,
and researched pipes.

00:17:45.933 --> 00:17:49.683
(audience applauding loudly)

00:17:53.686 --> 00:17:56.382
So I'm not for theoretical plumbers.

00:17:56.382 --> 00:17:57.215
(whoosh)

00:17:57.215 --> 00:17:58.048
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:17:58.048 --> 00:18:02.189
I made this video because
nickthegreek82 asked me to.

00:18:02.189 --> 00:18:03.953
So if there's a famous
entrepreneur that you want me

00:18:03.953 --> 00:18:06.243
to profile next, leave
it in the Comments below

00:18:06.243 --> 00:18:07.430
and I'll see what I can do.

00:18:07.430 --> 00:18:10.172
I'd also love to know
which of the Top 10 Rules

00:18:10.172 --> 00:18:12.750
was your favorite, had the
biggest impact on you and why.

00:18:12.750 --> 00:18:13.583
Leave it in the Comments

00:18:13.583 --> 00:18:14.885
and I'm going to join the discussion.

00:18:14.885 --> 00:18:16.276
Thank you so much for watching

00:18:16.276 --> 00:18:18.856
and continue to believe
and I'll see you soon.

00:18:18.856 --> 00:18:20.939
(whoosh)

